In Modern English, the letter {{m|en|b}} is sometimes silent, as when final after {{m|en|m}}, in {{m|en|climb}}, {{m|en|comb}}, {{m|en|lamb}}, {{m|en|limb}}, {{m|en|tomb}}, {{m|en|thumb}}, etc. and in a few other cases, such as {{m|en|debt}}, {{m|en|doubt}} and {{m|en|subtle}}. For some words ending in ''mb'' (e.g. {{m|en|limb}}, {{m|en|numb}}, {{m|en|thumb}}), the ''b'' was never pronounced and is a modern graphic insertion on the analogy of words like {{m|en|climb}}, {{m|en|comb}} and {{m|en|lamb}} where the ''b'' was in fact once pronounced. Likewise, the silent ''b'' in words in ''-bt-'' is an etymological insertion intended to bring the spelling closer to the Latin etymon; compare the original French words {{m|fr|doute||doubt}}, {{m|fr|dette||debt}} and {{m+|fro|soutil||subtle}} from which the English words were derived.<ref>{{R:Century|year=1914}}</ref>

